### Detailed Study of Java Utility Classes: StringTokenizer, Formatter, and Scanner

#### 1. Core Functions of Utility Classes:
Java provides specialized helper classes under `java.util` and `java.io` to parse, format, and interact with structured text without manual regex compilation.

#### 2. Detailed Technical Comparison Table:
| Utility Class | Package | Primary Functionality | Core Methods |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`StringTokenizer`** | `java.util` | Fast tokenization of strings using delimiter sets. | `hasMoreTokens()`, `nextToken()`, `countTokens()` |
| **`Formatter`** | `java.util` | Rich text formatting supporting alignment and dates. | `format()`, `flush()`, `close()` |
| **`Scanner`** | `java.util` | Parses formatted primitive inputs with delimiter scanning. | `hasNextInt()`, `nextInt()`, `nextLine()` |
| **`Console`** | `java.io` | Terminal interaction supporting secure password input. | `readLine()`, `readPassword()` |

#### 3. Complete Demonstrative Java Implementation:
```java
import java.util.StringTokenizer;
import java.util.Formatter;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class UtilityClassesDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. StringTokenizer: Parsing CSV record
        String csvData = "CS305PC;Java Programming;Semester-III;MGIT;Hyderabad";
        StringTokenizer tokenizer = new StringTokenizer(csvData, ";");

        System.out.println("Tokenizing CSV record (Total tokens: " + tokenizer.countTokens() + "):");
        while (tokenizer.hasMoreTokens()) {
            System.out.println("  -> " + tokenizer.nextToken());
        }

        // 2. Formatter: Precision text formatting
        StringBuilder buffer = new StringBuilder();
        try (Formatter formatter = new Formatter(buffer)) {
            formatter.format("%-15s %-10s %8.2f", "Amaan", "Distinction", 94.755);
            System.out.println("\nFormatted String Output:\n" + buffer);
        }

        // 3. Scanner: Token-based parsing
        String numericStream = "10 20 30 40 50";
        try (Scanner scanner = new Scanner(numericStream)) {
            int sum = 0;
            while (scanner.hasNextInt()) {
                sum += scanner.nextInt();
            }
            System.out.println("Sum parsed via Scanner: " + sum);
        }
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Java utility classes streamline stream parsing, tokenization, and formatted display.}$$
